.TH HI 1 "2026-09-06" "say-hi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hi \- your shell config, on every host you say hi to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hi
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RB [ \-\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-mux | \-\-no\-mux ]
.I target
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B hi
.br
.B hi \-\-doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B hi \-\-install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-link ]
.RB [ \-\-prefix " " \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B hi \-\-configure
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.br
.BR "hi \-\-uninstall" " | " \-\-update " [" \fItag\fR "]"
.br
.B hi \-\-preview
.RB { colors | packages | header }
.br
.BR "hi \-h" " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hi
copies your
.B say-hi
configuration tree (prompt, colors, aliases, vim/nano configs, connect
header) to a target, starts a session there, and removes every trace when
the session ends. It is sshrc, supercharged.
.PP
With no
.IR command ,
.B hi
opens an interactive session on the target (see
.B SESSION SHELL
for which shell). With a trailing
.IR command ,
it runs that command on the target and exits \- inside hi's session, not the
way
.BR ssh (1)
runs one: the command has hi's aliases, PATH and environment, and runs on a
pty when your own stdin is one. Only the command's own output goes to stdout;
hi's progress and errors go to stderr. For a plain, unstyled, pty\-free
remote command \- a tarball you are piping into a file, say \- use
.BR ssh (1)
itself.
.PP
The payload is base64\-armored through the target's login shell for ssh
targets, unpacked into a private
.BR mktemp (1)
directory, and removed by cleanup traps on exit \- including on an abrupt
disconnect. If the target has a permanent say-hi (installed with
.BR install.sh ,
anywhere \- the location is read from that target's login rc files, and failing
that from the standard install prefixes), that copy is used in place and left
alone.
.SH SESSION SHELL
The session runs in your login shell when hi styles it (bash, zsh or fish),
otherwise in the best of fish > zsh > bash. Both halves come off one
ordering \- fish > zsh > bash > dash > ash > sh \- so a target with no bash
at all falls back to the best of the rest of it: fish > zsh > dash > ash > sh,
with hi's aliases and colored prompt but no header.
.B _HI_SHELL_PREFERENCE
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT )
reorders the styled three.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
.I target
is tried against each backend, in order; the first match wins:
.TP
.B 1. ssh host
a literal
.B Host
entry in
.B ~/.ssh/config
(wildcard entries are not matched).
.TP
.B 2. container, through docker, podman, nerdctl or finch
a running container, by name or ID, on whichever member of
.B $_HI_CONTAINER_CLIS
answers, in that order.
.TP
.B 3. nomad allocation
a running allocation, by ID or ID prefix.
.TP
.B 4. kubernetes pod
a running pod in the current
.BR kubectl (1)
context and namespace, or
.I namespace:pod
and
.I context:namespace:pod
for one elsewhere \- the prefixes become
.B kubectl \-\-context
and
.BR "\-\-namespace" .
Completion spells a pod outside the current namespace the same way.
.PP
The last two can hold more than one thing to land in, and a
.B /
suffix says which:
.I alloc/task
becomes
.B nomad alloc exec \-task
and
.I pod/container
becomes
.BR "kubectl exec \-c" .
Without it the two differ:
.BR nomad (1)
refuses a multi-task exec outright, while
.BR kubectl (1)
falls back to the pod's first container and warns. Completion offers the
pairs for any allocation or pod that has more than one.
.PP
A name matching none of these is handed to
.BR ssh (1)
anyway, so unlisted hosts still work.
.PP
.BR \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR
before
.I target
names the arm outright and skips every probe above it \- the fix for a
container that shadows an unrelated ssh host of the same name, or the other
way around.
.SH NO TARGET
.B hi
with no
.I target
and no
.I ssh\-options
offers the list instead of failing \- the same rows the shell completions read,
so the offer is backend\-tagged, ranked by what you connected to recently, and
served out of the
.B $_HI_TARGETS_TTL
cache a
.B TAB
may already have warmed. Picking one connects to it.
.PP
.BR fzf (1)
is used when it is on the client, then
.BR sk (1);
with neither, a numbered
.B select
menu, so nothing has to be installed for this to work. The picker runs on the
client only and never reaches a target.
.PP
Both ends of a terminal are required, so
.B hi
in a script or a CI job goes on printing the usage message from
.BR ssh (1)
and failing rather than waiting on a menu nobody can answer. An
.I ssh\-option
with no target does the same: an option without a host is an error for
.BR ssh (1)
to report, not a target to guess at.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
print a usage summary \- the synopsis, the target resolution order, and hi's
own options \- and exit. Everything here in short form; this page is the long
one. Caught before the pass\-through below, so it never reaches
.BR ssh (1).
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
print the version and exit: the version stamped in at packaging time, or
.B git describe
output in a checkout. The one short
.BR ssh (1)
option hi claims for itself \- hi's version is the first thing a bug report
asks for, and
.B ssh \-V
is a keystroke away for the other one.
.TP
.B \-\-use \fIbackend\fR
force that arm for
.I target
and skip every probe in
.B TARGET RESOLUTION
above:
.B ssh
(ssh's own error speaks if the host is not there), or a backend by name \-
.BR docker ,
.BR podman ,
any other member of
.BR $_HI_CONTAINER_CLIS ,
.B nomad
or
.BR kube .
The name has to be one hi was loaded with; a stranger is an error, not a
guess.
.B \-\-use=\fIbackend\fR
is the same flag with its word joined.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
skip the payload entirely and hand over a bare shell on whichever arm
.I target
resolves to \- no copy, no aliases, no header. Needs nothing from the target
beyond a shell (and, for an ssh target,
.BR sshd (8))
\- not even
.BR tar (1)
or
.BR base64 (1),
and no writable
.B /tmp
or
.B $HOME
either.
.TP
.B \-\-mux
run the session inside a local
.BR tmux (1)
session named for the target
.RB ( hi\-<target> ,
with the characters tmux rejects turned into dashes), so a dropped link leaves
the session running here: the next
.B hi \-\-mux
to the same target reattaches instead of opening a second one. Entirely
client\-side \- the target sees the same disposable session as ever. From
inside a tmux, the session is created detached and this client switches to
it. Without a terminal, or without tmux on this machine, hi connects as usual.
.B _HI_MUX=1
in the settings makes it the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
connect without the tmux wrap, whatever
.B _HI_MUX
says \- the way out of that default for one connect. Of
.B \-\-mux
and
.BR \-\-no\-mux ,
the last one on the line wins.
.TP
.B \-\-preview \fR{\fBcolors\fR|\fBpackages\fR|\fBheader\fR}
print one of three things and exit, instead of connecting.
.B colors
is every ssh host and your user in their resolved colors, each row naming the
rule it matched \- a pin, a tag, a pattern or the hash.
.B packages
is the package\-priority legend, as the header's check prints it, with the
ranks the floor silences marked.
.B header
is the connect header itself, as it prints here at your settings.
.B colors
wants
.BR scripts/ ,
so inside a session it says so and stops; the other two have a full form
there too and fall back to the check and the header out of the shipped
.B common/header.sh
on a target.
.PP
The local commands act on this machine instead of connecting, and each needs
a part of the tree that is not always present. All of them want
.BR scripts/ ,
which a package\-manager install ships but a hi session does not \- so inside a
session each says so and stops.
.B \-\-update
wants
.B .git
on top of that, and a package carries none.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-use\fR \fIbackend\fR] [\fItarget\fR]
print a read\-only pre\-flight report instead of connecting: the local tree,
the config overlay, the shell rc files and the overlay's own shell files
parsed by whatever will read them (what
.B \-\-install
checks before it writes a line), every backend probed and timed, and \- with
a target \-
which backend the name resolves to plus an ssh reachability check.
.B \-\-use
reports the same forced backend a real connect with it would take, skipping
the probe chain. With
.BR \-\-json ,
the same rows as one JSON document on stdout \- what a bug report should
carry \- with the finding count in it as
.BR findings .
Exits 1 on any finding (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
.TP
.B \-\-install \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-link\fR] [\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR]
install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files, asking before each,
and seed the config overlay at
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
with the shipped defaults (files already there are never touched).
.B \-\-yes
answers every prompt yes;
.B \-\-no\-link
skips the
.B hi
symlink into
.BR /usr/bin ;
.B \-\-prefix
is packaging mode \- stage the tree under
.I dir
and write nothing to a home \- which the package builds use and a user never
needs. The script behind it,
.BR scripts/install.sh ,
also takes
.BR \-\-check\-configs ,
the same read\-only pre\-flight over your rc files and overlay that
.B \-\-doctor
reports, as an exit status alone.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
take those lines back out again. The tree itself is left where it is.
.TP
.B \-\-configure \fR[\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR]
revisit the settings only, leaving the rc wiring alone: a menu over a live
preview of the header and the prompt. Pick a preset (\fBeverything\fR,
\fBbalanced\fR, \fBminimal\fR), or open a section \- Header (the real header
rendered above a list of its items: toggle, reorder, presets, width, the
package check's depth and palette, the addresses the ip cell hides), Features,
Prompt, Advanced, Colors (a truecolor scheme) \- then
\fBs\fR saves and \fBq\fR leaves the file untouched; nothing is written
before either.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
applies one without the menu.
.TP
.B \-\-update \fR[\fItag\fR]
move the say-hi checkout to a release tag: the tags are fetched and the newest
is checked out, detached \- or the
.I tag
named, to move on or back. Releases are tags and nothing else; following a
branch is
.B git pull
in the checkout, by hand.
A working tree with uncommitted changes is refused before anything moves \-
commit or stash first.
Needs a checkout: an install from a package has no
.B .git
to move, and says so rather than running.
.PP
Everything else is passed through to
.BR ssh (1)
unchanged \-
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-o ,
and the rest behave exactly as they do there. Only the first
non\-option word is taken as the target; everything after it is the
remote command.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B a connect
the session's, or the remote command's, own exit status \- what
.BR ssh (1)
would hand back \- once the target has been cleaned up. 1 when hi itself
refused before connecting: a
.B \-\-use
name it was not loaded with, two of them, or a local command run inside a
session.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
0 with nothing to report, 1 on any finding. The count is in the report
(and in
.BR \-\-json 's
.BR findings ),
never in the status.
.TP
.B the other local commands
the script's own: 0 when it did what it says, 1 otherwise \- an rc file
that fails its syntax check stops
.B \-\-install
at 1 before it writes a line.
.SH COMPLETION
.B hi
completes in bash, zsh and fish. All three read one list \-
.B common/targets.sh
\- so the three can not drift apart, and it is the only one fish can run.
.TP
.B hi <TAB>
every target: the
.B Host
entries in
.B ~/.ssh/config
plus every running container, allocation and pod, each tagged with the
backend it came from. The list is reused for
.B $_HI_TARGETS_TTL
seconds and each backend gets
.B $_HI_PROBE_TIMEOUT
to answer, because this runs on every keypress.
.TP
.B hi \-\-<TAB>
hi's own options, each with its one\-line purpose where the shell has a
column for it (fish and zsh do; bash lists the names). Answered without
probing any backend at all. The list is
filtered to what this install can actually run: a session is offered none of
the local commands that need
.BR scripts/ ,
and a package\-manager install is not offered
.BR \-\-update ,
which needs
.BR .git .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives outside the tree, in
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
.RB ( settings.sh ,
.BR colors ,
.BR packages ,
.BR vim.rc
and
.BR nano.rc ,
additive
.BR aliases.sh ,
and per-shell
.BR bash.sh ,
.B zsh.zsh
and
.BR config.fish ),
written by
.B hi \-\-configure
and carried along to every target. Every feature is an environment
variable toggle \- header, prompt, git status, editors, clipboard \- each on
by default and tabulated in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md .
The tree itself can be
root\-owned (a package\-manager install): nothing hi does writes into it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/say-hi
the tree, for package\-manager installs (a git checkout at
.B ~/say-hi
works identically).
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh
feature toggles, written by
.BR "hi \-\-configure" .
.TP
.B /etc/say-hi/settings.sh
a platform team's defaults, sourced below each user's
.BR settings.sh ;
this machine only, never a target's.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/colors, ~/.config/say-hi/packages
per\-user overrides of the tree's defaults.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/vim.rc, ~/.config/say-hi/nano.rc
per\-user replacements of the tree's editor configs, used whole rather than
merged.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/aliases.sh
your own aliases, sourced first so any
.B _HI_*_OPTS
value or
.B _HI_DISABLE_*
toggle you set there wins over the shipped set - an
.B alias
of the same name does not, since the shipped one is defined after and
overwrites it.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/bash.sh, ~/.config/say-hi/zsh.zsh, ~/.config/say-hi/config.fish
your own per\-shell preferences, sourced after hi's own so yours win.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B _HI_HOME
the parent of the
.B say-hi
directory; set by
.B /etc/profile.d/say-hi.sh
for packaged installs.
.TP
.B _HI_SHELL_PREFERENCE
ordered list of session shells to try
.RB ( login
means your login shell); default
.BR login ,
then the shells hi styles in preference order:
.BR "fish zsh bash" .
.TP
.B _HI_CONTAINER_CLIS
the docker\-compatible CLIs a container is listed and reached through, in
order; default
.BR "docker podman nerdctl finch" .
Each is its own
.B \-\-use
name and its own completion tag; one that is not installed costs nothing.
.TP
.B _HI_MUX
.B 1
wraps every connect in the local
.BR tmux (1)
session
.B \-\-mux
describes;
.B \-\-no\-mux
skips it for one. Default
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B _HI_RECENT, _HI_RECENT_FILE
.B 1
(the default) records every target a session ended cleanly on, in
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/say-hi/recent
or wherever
.B _HI_RECENT_FILE
points, and completion and the bare\-hi picker offer those first;
.B 0
neither records nor ranks. Client\-side only.
.TP
.B _HI_TRUECOLOR
.B 1
or
.BR 0 :
whether the client's terminal renders 24\-bit color. Read off
.B COLORTERM
and shipped to the session, which ssh never forwards itself; set it to force
(inside tmux, say) or refuse a scheme's hex.
.TP
.B _HI_TARGETS_TTL, _HI_PROBE_TIMEOUT
completion's two knobs: seconds the target list is reused for (default 5)
and seconds each backend gets to answer (default 2), in completion and in
the connect header alike.
.TP
.B _HI_DISABLE_HEADER, _HI_DISABLE_PROMPT, ...
per\-feature opt\-outs; see
.I docs/SETTINGS.md
for the full table.
.TP
.B _HI_COLOR_SCHEME
render the twelve palette names as a truecolor scheme \-
.BR catppuccin ,
.BR monokai ,
.BR onedark ,
or
.BR vscode
\- wherever hi paints, on a terminal that reports 24\-bit color
.RB ( COLORTERM ;
.B _HI_TRUECOLOR=1
forces it, 0 refuses). Unset, the terminal's own sixteen colors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B hi prod-db
interactive session on the ssh host
.BR prod\-db ,
with your prompt, colors and aliases.
.TP
.B hi -J bastion internal 'df -h'
run a command on
.B internal
through a jump host.
.TP
.B hi 8f41c2
session inside a running container or allocation with that ID.
.TP
.B hi \-\-use docker web
the container named
.BR web ,
even though an ssh host of the same name exists \- no probe is run.
.TP
.B hi \-\-plain rescue
a bare shell on
.B rescue
with nothing copied: for a box with no writable
.B /tmp
or no
.BR tar (1).
.TP
.B hi \-\-doctor \-\-json prod-db > report.json
the pre\-flight report as a document, for a bug report; the exit status
says whether anything was found.
.TP
.B hi
no target: pick one from the list \- see
.B NO TARGET
above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Full documentation \- the toggle tables in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ,
the testing story in
.IR docs/TESTING.md ,
and the README over both:
.I https://github.com/ivylikethevine/say-hi
